    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm doing a quick accessibility audit on a new fintech app we're building. Can you give me a checklist of the key WCAG 2.1 requirements I should look for, specifically regarding color contrast ratios and screen reader compatibility for mobile? Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! For color contrast ratios, ensure they meet the minimum requirements of AA level: at least 4.5:1 for text and visual display elements, and 3:1 for large-scale images of text. Screen reader compatibility should be checked by ensuring that all interactive elements are properly labeled, using ARIA attributes, and having an accessible structure for easy navigation. It's also vital to include alternative text for images and forms to aid screen reader users. Remember to test both the app's front-end for color contrast and the back-end ARIA roles and labels as well. Don't forget to test with a screen reader to experience it from your user's perspective.",
