    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's helpful. To get a bit more specific, some of the edge cases are tripping us up—mostly those weird, intermittent bugs where icons don't load right or the screen reader skips a button entirely. What are some best practices for catching those kinds of hidden interface glitches during a testing phase?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To identify intermittent bugs, implement continuous testing throughout your development cycle. Use automated testing tools to simulate various user interactions, including widespread taps, swipes, and prolonged sessions to catch these erratic behaviors. Employ a variety of screen readers and assistive technologies, and perform manual testing alongside automated tests to get a human touch on complex interactions. Record user sessions to reenact situations where bugs were reported, and consider crowdsourcing your app testing with a diverse group of users, including those with disabilities. Remember, small mistakes in accessibility testing can significantly impact user experience—prioritize fixing these bugs even if they appear only in rare corner cases. Pay attention to the feedback from testing tools that flag up common accessibility issues and dig deeper into those reports. Lastly, set up proper logging to capture errors and exceptions, and it would be resilient if you can analyze and understand the conditions that lead to these intermittent bugs. By embracing a robust, iterative testing approach, these complex issues will become less of a headache.",
      "source": "target",
