    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Yeah, the \"headache\" part is real. It feels like these glitches just pop up out of nowhere, almost like they're intentionally trying to sabotage the user flow. Is there a way to categorize these kinds of \"sneaky\" bugs so the dev team knows exactly what they're dealing with?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely. Start by categorizing these bugs based on their nature and impact. For example, one group might be 'UI-related', involving problems with the visual representation of data and controls; another might be 'Accessibility-limited', where interactions are hindered by missing labels, synonyms, or inconsistencies. A third category could be 'Interaction-based', where the timing, sequence or user input triggers the malfunction.\n\nFor UI-related bugs, look into graphical rendering issues, mismatched styling, or problems with resolution. As for Accessibility-limited bugs, focus on elements that fail to convey their function or affordance, like interactive buttons or media, or those that disrupt navigation, like skip links. Interaction-based bugs might involve elements that mistakenly get closed, skipped, or aren't responsive to touch or clicks in a predictable manner.\n\nAfter categorizing, prioritize fixes based on the severity of the impact and the frequency of the bug occurrence across user scenarios. Continuous integration (CI) and deployment (CD) pipelines can be equipped with additional checks that specifically look for these categories of bugs, making sure that they are reduced before production release. When categorizing and prioritizing, it's also important to keep communication clear with all stakeholders; after all, these glitches can affect the perceived quality",
      "source": "target",
